====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q178794}} A [[portable]] or [[wearable]] [[timepiece]].
#: {{hyper|en|timepiece<id:Q376>|<|device#Noun}}
#: {{hypo|en|wristwatch|pocket watch|fob watch|stopwatch|quartz watch|smartwatch|digital watch<id:Q125454714>|analogue watch<id:Q485071>|analog watch<id:Q485071>|duplex watch|mystery watch}}
#: {{mer|en|watch face|watch receiver|watchspring|watch battery|watchstrap|watch strap|watchband}}
#: {{cot|en|clock#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|More people today carry a '''watch''' on their wrists than in their pockets; some people today don't use a '''watch''' at all, because their smartphone serves the purpose well enough.}} 
#: {{ux|en|He set the alarm on his '''watch''' to 8:00 am.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=2|passage=Sunning himself on the board steps, I saw for the first time Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke.{{...}}A silver snaffle on a heavy leather '''watch''' guard which connected the pockets of his corduroy waistcoat, together with a huge gold stirrup in his Ascot tie, sufficiently proclaimed his tastes.}}
# The act of guarding and observing someone or something.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=12|passage=shepherds keeping '''watch''' by night|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1717|author=w:Joseph Addison|title=Metamorphoses
|passage=All the long night their mournful '''watch''' they keep.}}
# A particular [[time]] period when [[guard]]ing is kept.
#: {{ux|en|The second '''watch''' of the night began at midnight.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|V|v|passage=I did stand my '''watch''' upon the hill.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Comus|passage=Might we but hear {{...}} <br> Or whistle from the lodge, or village cock <br> Count the night '''watches''' to his feathery dames.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|title=Mountain Charley|author=E. J. Guerin|page=30|text=In the evening a tremendous thunder storm, accompanied by wind and rain. It is my '''watch''' and I find it a terrible time to act as sentry.}}
# A period of wakefulness between the two sleeps of a [[biphasic sleep]] pattern (the [[dead sleep]] or [[first sleep]] and [[morning sleep]] or [[second sleep]]): the first waking.
# A person or group of people who [[guard]].
#: {{ux|en|The '''watch''' stopped the travelers at the city gates.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|27|65|passage=Pilate said unto them, Ye have a '''watch'''; go your way, make it as sure as ye can.}}
# The post or office of a watchman; also, the place where a watchman is posted, or where a guard is kept.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|V|ii|passage=He upbraids Iago, that he made him <br> Brave me upon the '''watch'''.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A group of sailors and officers aboard a ship or shore station with a common period of duty: ''starboard watch'', ''port watch''.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[period]] of [[time]] on [[duty]], usually four hours in length; the officers and crew who [[tend]] the [[working]] of a [[vessel]] during the same watch. (FM 55&ndash;501).
# The act of seeing, or viewing, for a period of time.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Charles P. Nemeth|title=Criminal law|passage=A quick '''watch''' of Stanley Kubrick's Clockwork Orange sends this reality home fast. Amoral, vacuous, cold-blooded, unsympathetic, and chillingly evil describe only parts of the story.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=11 August 2016|author=Andrew Bullock|title=David Brent REVIEW: Life on the Road goes from painfully funny to just plain painful. Ouch|journal=Sunday Express
|passage=The first third of the film is laugh after laugh; {{...}} But half an hour in and this movie gets unnervingly dark and is an uncomfortable '''watch''' at times.}}
# {{lb|en|rare|collective}} A group of [[nightingale]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=sirens were singing like a '''watch''' of nightingales|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Works_of_John_Galsworthy/m1tPsBog4IQC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22watch+of+nightingales%22+-swarm+-asses+-gaggle+-bitterns&pg=PA419&printsec=frontcover|page=419|title=The Works of John Galsworthy|volume=16|author=John Galsworthy|year=1921|publisher=Charles Scribner's Sons}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=it was as if sunshine and a '''watch''' of nightingales had come into a sombre forest|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Here_Comes_an_Old_Sailor/Seget6B0Eu8C?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22watch+of+nightingales%22+-swarm+-asses+-gaggle+-bitterns&pg=PA267&printsec=frontcover|page=267|title=Here Comes an Old Sailor|author=Alfred Tresidder Sheppard|year=1927}}

