# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[look]] at, [[see]], or [[view]] for a period of time.
#: {{ux|en|'''Watching''' the clock will not make time go faster.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm tired of '''watching''' TV.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=10|passage=It was a joy to snatch some brief respite, and find himself in the rectory drawing–room. Listening here was as pleasant as talking; just to '''watch''' was pleasant. The young priests who lived here wore cassocks and birettas; their faces were fine and mild, yet really strong, like the rector's face; and in their intercourse with him and his wife they seemed to be brothers.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[observe]] over a period of [[time]]; to [[notice]] or pay [[attention]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Watch''' this!}}
#: {{ux|en|Put a little baking soda in some vinegar and '''watch''' what happens.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[mind]], [[attend]], or [[guard]].
#: {{ux|en|Please '''watch''' my suitcase for a minute.}}
#: {{ux|en|He has to '''watch''' the kids that afternoon.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1899|author=w:Stephen Crane| title=s:Twelve O'Clock| chapter=1| passage={{...}} (it was the town's humour to be always gassing of phantom investors who were likely to come any moment and pay a thousand prices for everything) &mdash; “{{...}} Them rich fellers, they don't make no bad breaks with their money. They '''watch''' it all th' time b'cause they know blame well there ain't hardly room fer their feet fer th' pikers an' tin-horns an' thimble-riggers what are layin' fer 'em.&nbsp;{{...}}”}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To be [[wary]] or [[cautious]] of.
#: {{ux|en|You should '''watch''' that guy. He has a reputation for lying.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To attend to dangers to or regarding.
#: {{ux|en|'''watch''' your head; '''watch''' your step}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Watch''' yourself when you talk to him.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Watch''' what you say.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To remain [[awake]] with a sick or dying person; to maintain a [[vigil]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1687|author=John Aubrey|title=Remaines of Gentilisme and Judaisme|page=30|text=At the funeralls in Yorkeshire, to this day, they continue the custome of '''watching''' & sitting-up all night till the body is interred.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[vigilant]] or [[on one's guard]].
#: {{ux|en|For some must '''watch''', while some must sleep: So runs the world away.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To act as a [[lookout]].
#* {{RQ:Bramah Eyes of Max Carrados|passage="{{...}} Scarcely a night passes without the light showing, and there's no doubt that the water runs. We've put copying ink on the door handles and the taps and got into it ourselves until there isn't a man about the place that you couldn't implicate."<br>"Has anyone '''watched''' up there?"}}
# {{lb|en|nautical|of a [[buoy]]}} To serve the purpose of a [[watchman]] by floating properly in its place.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To be [[awake]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1485|author=w:Thomas Malory|title=w:Le Morte d'Arthur|section=Book X
|passage=So on the morne Sir Trystram, Sir Gareth and Sir Dynadan arose early and went unto Sir Palomydes chambir, and there they founde hym faste aslepe, for he had all nyght '''wacched''' {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To be on the lookout for; to wait for expectantly.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1789|author={{w|John Moore (Scottish physician)|John Moore}}|title=Zeluco|publisher=Valancourt|year_published=2008|page=80
|passage=[S]he had reason to dread that her husband had formed a very criminal project of being revenged on Zeluco, and '''watched''' an opportunity of putting it in execution.}}

